            "The <strong>puerperium</strong> is the six weeks after birth in which the reproductive organs return to their non-pregnant state and the woman recovers physically and emotionally — and it is also the period in which <strong>most maternal deaths occur</strong>, more than half of them in the first 24 hours and most of the rest in the first week, from haemorrhage, sepsis, hypertension and embolism. The postnatal period is where coverage falls: nearly all Ghanaian women receive antenatal care, about four fifths deliver in a facility, and far fewer receive a postnatal check in the first days at home, where the dangers are. <strong>Involution</strong>: immediately after birth the uterus weighs about 1 kg and its fundus is at the umbilicus, firm and central; it descends about <strong>1 cm (a finger's breadth) a day</strong>, is at the symphysis by about day 7 and is <strong>no longer palpable abdominally by about day 10–12</strong> (the standard answer is day 10), reaching its non-pregnant 60 g by six weeks. <strong>Afterpains</strong> — cramps as the uterus contracts, stronger in multiparae and during breastfeeding — are normal and respond to paracetamol. A uterus that is <strong>high, soft and deviated to the right</strong> on day 2 has a full bladder beneath it; a uterus that is bulky, tender and slow to involute (subinvolution) with offensive lochia has retained products or infection.",
