            "Of every 1,000 babies born alive in Ghana about <strong>24 die in the first month</strong>, most in the first week and most of those on the first day — from <strong>birth asphyxia, prematurity and infection</strong>, and the essential newborn care that prevents most of these deaths costs almost nothing. The baby arrives from 37 °C into a room at 25, wet, with a large head and little fat, and loses heat by <strong>evaporation</strong> from wet skin, <strong>conduction</strong> to a cold surface, <strong>convection</strong> from a draught or a fan, and <strong>radiation</strong> to cold walls and windows; a cold baby uses up its glucose and oxygen trying to stay warm, becomes hypoglycaemic, acidotic and breathless, and dies of 'infection' or 'asphyxia' that was really cold. So the sequence begins before the birth: <strong>a warm room (at least 25 °C), fans and air conditioning off, doors closed, two warm dry cloths, a hat, the resuscitation area checked</strong> — bag and mask of the right sizes (0 for preterm, 1 for term), suction device, a clock, a flat warm surface, a light — the cord ties, the chlorhexidine gel, the eye ointment and the vitamin K drawn up, and a helper identified.",
            "<strong>At birth</strong>: note the time; <strong>dry the baby immediately and thoroughly</strong> with the first cloth, head first, while <strong>assessing breathing</strong> — drying is also stimulation; <strong>discard the wet cloth</strong>; if the baby is crying or breathing well, place it <strong>prone, skin-to-skin on the mother's abdomen or chest</strong>, cover both with the second dry cloth and put the <strong>hat</strong> on. Skin-to-skin keeps the baby warm better than any cot, steadies the heart rate and breathing, colonises the baby with the mother's rather than the ward's bacteria, prompts the first feed and begins attachment — the baby should stay there, uninterrupted, for at least the first hour, with weighing, measuring, eye care and vitamin K done afterwards or on the mother's chest. <strong>Delayed cord clamping</strong>: wait <strong>1–3 minutes</strong>, or until the cord stops pulsating, before clamping — the baby receives up to a third more blood, which prevents anaemia for months and helps the preterm baby's circulation; the exceptions are the baby who needs resuscitation that cannot be given on the mother (clamp and cut at once) and, in the mother, a heavy bleed. <strong>Tie or clamp the cord</strong> with a sterile tie or clamp about 2–3 cm from the abdomen and a second tie further out, cut between with a sterile blade, check for bleeding, and leave the stump <strong>exposed and dry</strong>. <strong>Early initiation of breastfeeding within the first hour</strong>: help the mother recognise the baby's readiness — rooting, mouthing, crawling towards the breast — and attach, without forcing; the colostrum is the first immunisation. <strong>Nothing is given by mouth but breast milk</strong> — no water, no glucose, no formula, no herbal preparation.",
            "<strong>Within the first hour (on the mother)</strong>: <strong>eye care</strong> — <strong>tetracycline 1% eye ointment</strong>, a line along the inside of each lower lid, once, to prevent gonococcal and chlamydial conjunctivitis which can blind in days; <strong>vitamin K</strong> — <strong>1 mg intramuscularly into the anterolateral thigh</strong> (0.5 mg if the baby weighs under 1.5 kg) to prevent haemorrhagic disease of the newborn, which bleeds from the gut, the cord, the skin and the brain in the first days and weeks because the newborn's clotting factors are low and breast milk contains little vitamin K; <strong>cord care</strong> — Ghana policy is <strong>7.1% chlorhexidine digluconate gel (4% chlorhexidine) applied once daily to the stump and its base from birth until the cord separates</strong> (about day 7–10), and otherwise nothing — <strong>no methylated spirit, no powder, no herbs, no ash, no cow dung, no toothpaste, no shea butter</strong>, which cause omphalitis and tetanus; the nappy is folded below the cord to keep it dry. <strong>Identification</strong> (two name bands, the mother's name, sex, time of birth) before the baby leaves her; <strong>weight, length and head circumference</strong> after the first hour; <strong>temperature</strong> (36.5–37.5 °C axillary) checked at one hour and before discharge; and <strong>immunisations before discharge</strong> — <strong>BCG</strong> intradermally into the left upper arm, <strong>oral polio vaccine zero dose</strong> and, in Ghana's schedule, the <strong>hepatitis B birth dose within 24 hours</strong>. The baby of an <strong>HIV-positive mother</strong> starts nevirapine (or the dual prophylaxis) within 72 hours and is tested at 6 weeks; the baby of a mother with <strong>untreated syphilis</strong> is treated; the baby of a mother with <strong>hepatitis B</strong> receives the birth dose urgently (and immunoglobulin where available). <strong>Bathing is delayed for at least 24 hours</strong> (six hours at the very earliest) to protect warmth and the vernix; the baby is wiped if soiled. The baby is <strong>not separated from the mother</strong> (rooming-in) and is <strong>examined fully before 24 hours</strong> and again before discharge.",
