            "The Apgar score (7–10 normal; 4–6 moderately depressed; 0–3 severely depressed) <strong>describes the baby's condition and response to resuscitation; it does not decide whether to resuscitate</strong> — a baby who is not breathing at 30 seconds is ventilated at 30 seconds, not scored at one minute — and a low one-minute score that is 8 or more at five minutes is reassuring, whereas a <strong>five-minute score below 7 is recorded, repeated at 10 minutes and referred for observation</strong>. A baby who at one minute has a heart rate of 90, a weak cry, some flexion, a grimace and a pink body with blue hands scores 1+1+1+1+1 = 5; a baby at five minutes who is pink with a heart rate of 140, crying lustily, actively moving and sneezing at the suction bulb scores 10. Peripheral cyanosis alone, in a baby that is otherwise well, is normal in the first day; <strong>central cyanosis, grunting, a respiratory rate over 60, chest indrawing, apnoea, a heart rate under 100 or persistently over 180, floppiness, and a temperature outside 36.5–37.5</strong> are not.",
            "The <strong>full newborn examination</strong> is done within the first 24 hours, in a warm room, in front of the mother, in good light, with the history (gestation, labour, membranes, risk factors for infection, maternal HIV, syphilis and hepatitis B status, drugs, family history) in mind, from head to toe and then reflexes: <strong>measurements</strong> — weight (term average 2.5–4.0 kg; <strong>under 2.5 kg is low birth weight</strong>, under 1.5 very low), length (about 50 cm), head circumference (about 33–35 cm; large or small, or a head growing faster than the chart, is referred); <strong>general</strong> — posture (flexed), tone, colour (jaundice in the first 24 hours is always abnormal), cry, activity, respiratory rate counted for a full minute (30–60), heart rate (120–160), temperature; <strong>head</strong> — the anterior fontanelle soft and flat (sunken: dehydration; bulging: raised pressure, meningitis), sutures, moulding and <strong>caput succedaneum</strong> (soft, crosses sutures, resolves in days) versus <strong>cephalhaematoma</strong> (firm, limited by a suture, takes weeks and may deepen jaundice); <strong>face and eyes</strong> — symmetry (facial palsy after forceps), red reflex where possible, discharge (sticky eye versus the purulent swollen lids of gonococcal ophthalmia, which is an emergency), subconjunctival haemorrhages (harmless); <strong>ears</strong> (position, skin tags, pits), <strong>mouth</strong> (cleft lip and palate — feel the palate with a finger; a tongue-tie that restricts feeding; white patches that do not wipe away are thrush), <strong>neck</strong> (swellings, a clavicle felt for crepitus after shoulder dystocia); <strong>chest</strong> (shape, indrawing, breath sounds, breast buds in both sexes are normal; a murmur is referred); <strong>abdomen</strong> (soft, liver edge 1–2 cm normal, cord with two arteries and one vein and no redness at the base, masses, distension); <strong>genitalia</strong> — male: both testes in the scrotum, the urethral opening at the tip (hypospadias is not circumcised), a hydrocele is common; female: labia, a white or slightly bloody discharge is maternal hormone withdrawal; ambiguous genitalia are referred before any sex is announced; <strong>anus</strong> patent — <strong>meconium passed within 24 hours and urine within 24 hours (some say 48)</strong>; <strong>back</strong> — the spine run with a finger for a dimple, tuft or swelling (spina bifida); <strong>hips</strong> — Ortolani and Barlow for dislocation (breech babies especially); <strong>limbs</strong> — count digits, palmar creases, club foot (talipes: refer early for casting), movement of both arms (an arm that lies limp and internally rotated is Erb's palsy); <strong>skin</strong> — vernix, lanugo, milia, erythema toxicum (red blotches with central white papules, harmless), Mongolian blue spots over the sacrum (normal, documented so they are not mistaken for bruising), birthmarks, pustules (infection — refer), peeling in the post-term baby; and the <strong>reflexes</strong> — rooting, sucking, grasp, Moro (symmetrical; asymmetry suggests a fractured clavicle or Erb's palsy), stepping, and tone in ventral suspension. Findings are recorded in the child health record book, explained to the mother, and anything outside the normal is referred that day.",
